Opt-in via `model.anthropic_runtime: claude_code` (api_mode `claude_code`), the
Anthropic-side twin of the Codex app-server runtime. Claude Code owns the tool
loop, permissions, hooks, MCP servers, skills and context compaction; Hermes is
the shell around it: sessions DB, gateway platforms, slash commands, memory.

- agent/transports/claude_code_session.py: one long-lived `claude -p
  --input-format stream-json --output-format stream-json` process per agent,
  `--session-id` on first start and `--resume` when the transcript exists (id
  derived from the Hermes session id, so a gateway session survives restarts);
  assistant / tool_result events projected into Hermes' message shape; the
  `result` event ends the turn with text, usage and cost; control_request
  interrupt; login-shell env only, explicit passthrough for provider keys.
- agent/claude_code_runtime.py: turn driver mirroring codex_runtime (shared
  helpers imported), event bridge to tool_progress / task-card callbacks and
  interim commentary, usage and cost accounting.
- Wiring: api mode registered, runtime_provider rewrite from config,
  conversation_loop dispatch, facade forward, interrupt drain, agent close,
  compression no-op (the CLI compacts its own transcript).
- Docs page + sidebar; tests against a fake stream-json `claude`.

Verified live against Claude Code with a real session: tool turn, follow-up,
close, and a resumed process that still remembered the earlier turn.

